            <div xml:id="d34">

                <pb facs="0144" n="32" sameAs="frus:frus1907p1/pg32"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 7661/29–30.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Chargé Wilson to the Secretary of State</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Buenos
                                Aires</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1907-11-06"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">November 6, 1907</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 638.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to my dispatch No. 629, of
                    October 16 last, and to previous correspondence on the same subject, I have the
                    honor to inclose herewith duplicate copies of the “Boletin del Ministerio de
                    Relaciones Exteriores,” No. 107, upon page 196 of which publication will be
                    found the correspondence between the British legation in this city and the
                    Argentine Government concerning the presence of boric acid in imported animal
                    food products, and explaining the chemical processes employed in the government
                    laboratories to discover the presence of this acid.</p>
                <p>I also inclose duplicate copies of a translation of the article in question, as
                    it may be of interest, in view of the difficulties raised by the Argentine
                    Government in regard to the importation of Underwood’s deviled ham.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles S.
                    Wilson.</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d34-a1.xml" xpointer="d34-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
